# Hostile audit — defect-face outer directional anchoring

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Status:** audited-working proof-only material; theorem state unchanged  
**Audited source:** [defect-face host components require outer directional anchoring](target_c_face_host_anchor_rank_2026-09-12.md)

## Verdict

The component anchoring inequality
'sum_j(2-r_j) <= dim ker(C_face transpose) <= s_Z' is valid once the
train closure matrix and exact corank identity are in force. It is a finite dual
statement: a bounded-face train component with no outer anchor costs two kernel
dimensions, and one with only one projective anchor costs one. The result does not
supply a quantitative lower mass for the second direction and does not close C-II.

## Checks

1. The face variables are two-dimensional vectors, and each train joining two distinct
   bounded faces contributes opposite blocks while a train joining a bounded face to the
   outer face contributes one grounded block. A bridge occurring twice on one bounded
   face has opposite signs and contributes zero, so it is not an artificial anchor.

2. For a connected bounded-face component K_j, assigning one vector t_j to every face
   in K_j and zero elsewhere annihilates every internal train column. Requiring t_j to
   be orthogonal to the span of the outer-anchor vectors leaves a space of dimension
   '2-r_j'. Different K_j use disjoint face blocks, so their kernel subspaces are
   independent. This proves the lower bound on the transpose kernel.

3. The upper bound uses the audited exact identity
   'dim ker(C_face transpose)=2f-rank(C_face)=sigma_C<=s_Z'. No new positivity,
   asymptotic, or carrier assumption is introduced.

4. The result keeps train endpoints as physical occurrences and leaves outer-to-outer
   trains outside the bounded-face closure matrix. Contact-isolated source points are
   not inserted into any matrix.

## Limits

- The statement excludes nontrivial same-bounded-face train returns through the
  audited post-126 train-loop lemma; it does not infer that exclusion from the
  scalar ledger.
- The integer rank condition does not imply a uniform angular separation or a positive
  density of secondary outer anchors. Near-collinear anchors can still have rank two.
- The theorem applies to bounded-face components in the full contact core, not to the
  Type-I inactive-container branch, and it does not establish a source-preserving
  diameter descent.

**Claim classification:** audited-working exact dual obstruction; proof-only research
candidate; C-II remains open.

